### Demo Script

## OPSEC Validator - 3 Minute Demo Script 
---

### SETUP (before audience arrives)
- Have the demo loaded with "Patrol SITREP" sample ready
- Model already warmed up (run one analysis beforehand)

---

### OPENING (30 sec)

*[Gesture to the screen]*

"OPSEC review today is manual. Analysts read communications, apply checklists, flag what they catch. It works—but it's slow. So you can only screen a fraction of what actually goes out.

And your people can't use AI tools like ChatGPT for this, because sensitive data can't go to the cloud. So they're stuck choosing between security and speed.

This demo shows a third option: AI-powered OPSEC review that runs entirely on-premises. Speed of AI, nothing leaves your network."

---

### THE DEMO (1.5 min)

*[Click "Analyze & Compare" - results should take ~90 sec, talk through it]*

"Here's a deployment email—the kind of thing someone might send to family. On the left, we're simulating what a generic AI tool would return. On the right, a purpose-built OPSEC analyzer."

*[While waiting, or after results load]*

**Point to LEFT panel (Consumer AI Tool):**

"The generic approach finds 'sensitive information'—names, dates, locations. But it's just a list. No categorization. No severity. No recommendations. An analyst still has to figure out what matters and what to do about it."

**Point to RIGHT panel (Mission-Built Analyzer):**

"Now look at the mission-built analyzer. Same email, completely different output:

- **UNIT_ID: CRITICAL** — '42nd Infantry Division' with a recommendation to use generic descriptors
- **LOCATION: CRITICAL** — 'Camp Arifjan, Kuwait' flagged as deployment destination
- **TIMING: CRITICAL** — 'March 15th' identified as operational schedule
- **CAPABILITIES: HIGH** — '12 Stryker vehicles, 180 personnel' tagged as force composition

Every finding is categorized by DoD OPSEC doctrine, rated by severity, and includes specific remediation guidance."

---

### THE BUSINESS CASE (45 sec)

"Three things matter here:

**One** — This runs entirely on the HP ZGX Nano. Nothing leaves your network. No cloud. Works in classified environments, works at the edge, works air-gapped.

**Two** — No per-token costs. Run a thousand analyses a day, the cost is the same. That changes the math—you can screen everything, not just spot-check.

**Three** — This took a week to build. The model is open source. The hardware is commercial off-the-shelf. You're not buying a decade-long contract—you're buying a capability you own and control."

---

### CLOSE (15 sec)

"This is what on-premises AI enables: capabilities that were impossible when the only option was cloud. Happy to show you the other samples or talk about how this fits your environment."

---

### IF THEY ASK QUESTIONS

**"How accurate is it?"**
> "It's not perfect—no AI is. But it catches things humans miss, especially aggregation risks where individual facts seem harmless but combine into a problem. The goal is augmenting analysts, not replacing them."

**"What about classified environments?"**
> "Everything runs on-premises. The model weights are open source and can go through your security review. No internet connection required after initial setup."

**"How hard is this to customize?"**
> "The OPSEC categories are defined in configuration—no model retraining needed. If you want to add organization-specific rules or adjust severity thresholds, that's a config change, not a development project."

**"What's the hardware cost?"**
> "The HP ZGX Nano runs around $3,500. Compare that to cloud API costs at scale—if you're screening hundreds of communications per day, the hardware pays for itself quickly."

**"Is this something we can buy?"**
> "This is a demo showing what's possible. The hardware is available now. Building something production-ready for your specific environment is a conversation we'd want to have with your team."
